WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.013
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:02.750
Sal Khan here from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.680
Welcome to the the Homeroom livestream.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.710
You might notice I've
upgraded my equipment

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:09.970
at Philippe's request.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.270
And so this is like now in HD.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.840
So this is a very,
very, very exciting day.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.740
So we have a very exciting guest today.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.480
We have Mayim Bialik,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.670
who is an actress, a neuroscientist,

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.880
now an author and a podcast creator,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.270
and we'll talk a lot about that.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.480
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.620
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.050
A reminder that we are,

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.760
Khan Academy is a not-for-profit.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.220
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.820
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.670
and making a donation.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.690
I want to also give a special shout out

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.130
to several organizations that stepped up,

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.950
especially when they realized
that during the pandemic

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.670
more people than ever were
relying on Khan Academy.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:52.680
And that's awesome,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.320
but it was also driving our costs up,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.153
our server costs.

00:00:55.153 --> 00:00:56.970
We wanted to accelerate
a bunch of content.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.160
So special thanks to Bank of
America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.410
Novartis, Fastly and General Motors,

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.020
And last but not least,

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.850
I want to give everyone a reminder

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.015
that there's a version of this livestream

00:01:10.015 --> 00:01:12.460
that is available in podcast form

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.330
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:18.110
Homeroom with Sal The Podcast.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.087
So with that, I am very
excited to introduce Mayim.

00:01:22.087 --> 00:01:23.463
Mayim, welcome.

00:01:24.306 --> 00:01:27.507
- Thank you, I hope I'm
not late to homeroom.

00:01:27.507 --> 00:01:28.340
- You're not.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.870
We need to get a bell so that people like,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.380
you know, they're in
mentally the right place.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.100
So there's so much that we can chat about,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.240
I'm trying to level it
so that our shoulders

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.835
are on the same level.
- I appreciated it.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:44.110
I felt like I was looking down at you.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:45.765
- Which you can do.

00:01:45.765 --> 00:01:48.860
So there's so much to talk about.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:51.770
We've gotten to know each
other virtually over several,

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.660
we were on a panel together
a couple of months ago

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.010
and then I was on your podcast yesterday

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.490
and there's a lot of stuff to overlap,

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.130
but I did tell you when I first met you

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.120
and I'm not normally like into
this whole celebrity thing,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.100
but there's something about
growing up watching "Blossom",

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.560
and I know you've done other
things in your life since then

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.510
and you've gotten Phd's,
you're a neuroscientist,

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.010
but as I said yesterday,

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.160
I try to play it cool when I'm around you.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.970
- Well I think I told
you the first time we met

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:23.970
when we were on that panel together,

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.250
you are the celebrity to me.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.940
My kids have been using
Khan Academy for years

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.040
and it's an enormously
important part of our lives

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.750
and when I told my kids

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.080
that I was gonna be on a panel with you,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.870
we all freaked out.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.323
So it is really my honor
to be in Homeroom with you.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.320
- Well sounds like a win-win then.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.950
Well, there's so much to talk about

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:51.670
and I encourage anyone watching
to put your questions in.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.340
We have team members who
can surface the questions

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.680
to myself and Mayim.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.570
But you have an eclectic background.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:01.980
There are not a lot of
neuroscientist author

00:03:03.721 --> 00:03:06.430
actresses in the world.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.020
How do you identify?

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:10.540
Do you connect with all
of those identities?

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.600
Are you more one than the other?

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.210
Did you think you would
have this hodgepodge

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:17.390
of things to do?

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.380
- No, I mean I identify as
the grandchild of immigrants.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.840
Like when I think of who I am,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.600
you know, my grandparents
came to this country

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.250
during the pogroms leading
up to the Holocaust.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.160
So that's, I'm my parent's daughter

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.950
and I'm my grandparent's grandchild

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:41.950
and everything that happened
once they hit Ellis Island

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.510
is really my story.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.440
Everybody's got a story.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.330
For me, I was raised in a very colorful

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.320
first-generation American household

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.610
and my native language is Yiddish,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.780
that's the language of
Eastern European Jews.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.430
I was raised really
spanning several cultures

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.430
and I was a very
problem-solving kind of child

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.510
and I felt very comfortable on a stage

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.890
and I became an actor, you
know, as a young person,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.820
but truly I'm a thinker

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.430
and I found math and science late in life

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.100
and fought really hard to
be able to be a scientist.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.600
When it doesn't come naturally to you

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.170
and you're told that if
it doesn't come naturally,

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:26.090
it's not for you,

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.010
it's very hard to try and
break into that at 15 and 16,

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.280
which is what I did.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.250
So I became a neuroscientist.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.840
It took, you know, 12 years

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.730
between undergrad and grad school

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.150
and I had my first son in grad school.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.270
He's now 15 and I have a 12 year old.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.380
So I'm a mom,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.660
like that's really what
my life is, I'm a mom.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.830
And yes, I have written books,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.273
I do a lot of things,

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.480
not to make ends meet,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.540
but to sort of satisfy

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.610
the different parts of my
brain and my life and yeah,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.030
I guess I'm all of those hyphenates.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.040
I'm also a lactation educator counselor.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.910
I've been that for about 13 years.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.620
And so I speak to women about
breastfeeding and labor,

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.190
like I do a lot of different things

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.740
'cause I just feel like
that's my human experience.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.720
- Well, it's fascinating.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.079
So I mean take us on your
journey a little bit.

00:05:16.079 --> 00:05:18.150
You know, a lot of people
watching are young people

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.900
trying to figure out what
to do with their lives.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.220
You at a very young age,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.203
I mean how did you get into acting?

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.090
Was your family into it?

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.900
- No, my parents were
public school teachers

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.580
and documentary filmmakers.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.040
My parents were both English teachers.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.163
My mother was a preschool
early childhood educator.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.900
I didn't grow up in a Hollywood house.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.520
I grew up in kind of a crummy
rental house in Hollywood

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.450
and was part of the busing
program of the '70s and '80s

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.230
that took kids from rougher neighborhoods

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.710
and took us to neighborhoods

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.410
where there were more
educational opportunities.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.360
So I grew up just like a normal kid

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.840
just living with parents
who were teachers.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.030
And I was in school plays
in elementary school

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.520
and really liked it,

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.300
felt safe on a stage.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.280
I wasn't one of those
like super hammy kids,

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.100
like look at me, I'm amazing,

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.350
like I didn't think that

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.930
I just knew that there was
a safety for me that I felt

00:06:17.908 --> 00:06:20.340
honestly when making other people happy.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.630
And there's a very interesting psychology

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:25.150
to why some actors really do act

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:27.280
because we want other
people to feel something.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.160
And that was kind of
what it was like for me.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.820
And I knew that there were kid actors.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:34.120
It didn't occur to me that
no one looked like me in 1986

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.990
and that maybe I wouldn't have success.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.740
I indeed did not have success

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.990
in traditional things that
kid actors do like commercials

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:44.250
because back then there was
something called all American

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.550
and I didn't look like it.

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:49.590
- So how did you fall into that?

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.150
Did someone just discover you?

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.030
Was there an ad you read?

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:59.850
- No, it's literally like
the 1986 story is that

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.270
my parents typed a letter on a typewriter

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:03.410
and took a picture of me

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.750
like just in the backyard, a cute headshot

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.070
and they mailed it.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.123
There was no computers that we had then.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:11.270
There wasn't anything to email.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:14.270
They looked in the yellow pages
and just found kids agents

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.660
and literally sent my
picture and this letter

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:19.040
to nine different kids agents.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.240
And I ended up picking an agent
who happened to have a lot,

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.920
quite famous kids have
come out of her agency,

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.010
Neil Patrick Harris and Johnny Galecki,

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.650
who I was with on "The Big Bang Theory"

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.910
and Mario Lopez was with her.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.420
So I ended up just going on auditions

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.680
and I was in a lot of
sitcoms from the '80s,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.520
things like "Webster",
"MacGyver", "Facts of Life".

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.400
And then I was cast to
play the young Bette Midler

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.610
in "Beaches" when I was 12

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.300
and it came out when I was 13.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.243
And then after that, I did
"Blossom" from 14 to 19.

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:55.562
- I have to find these clips.

00:07:55.562 --> 00:07:56.395
You've just like,

00:07:56.395 --> 00:08:00.775
my entire childhood just came
back to me in that one answer.

00:08:00.775 --> 00:08:02.110
- I mean my head didn't change size.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:04.370
I had this enormous head,
I had a little twig body

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.593
and then I just sort of grew into my head.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:09.730
- I have a similar experience.

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:12.140
Actually, I'm I'm three
inches taller than my wife

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.900
but our shoulders are at the same height.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.960
So I'm all head and I've always been.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.230
And I see it in my children,

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:21.569
like when they're born,

00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:22.402
they can't touch the top of their heads

00:08:22.402 --> 00:08:25.293
because their heads are
the same size as mine.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.110
But your parents,

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.060
they must've seen something
in you at that age

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.260
to kind of go through the
trouble of taking your headshot

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.908
and sending it in.

00:08:33.908 --> 00:08:35.563
- Yeah I think, look,

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.280
I was raised in a traditional Jewish house

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.230
and there is a lot of performance

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:44.170
to a lot of aspects of our culture,

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:46.230
meaning when we read the Torah,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.300
which is the Old Testament, it's chanted

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.280
and a bar mitzvah's a very presentational,

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.480
we sing our Bible, like
we are a presentation,

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:58.280
Passover involves reciting
the four questions

00:08:58.280 --> 00:08:59.480
and being able to do that.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.650
So I definitely had a lot of opportunities

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:05.510
and I was in dance as a lot of
young kids are, young girls.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.720
So I was a ballerina
from the time I was four,

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:11.670
I was in classical English
ballet and I danced

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:12.980
and sure I was charismatic,

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.710
but I think a lot of
parents think their kids

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:16.890
are cute and charismatic.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:17.723
I don't know,

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:20.890
they said they always
felt like I had something

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.610
but I really don't know.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.656
'Cause like my mom still is like

00:09:24.656 --> 00:09:25.850
"You're prettier than
any woman in the world."

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.643
And it's like, I know that's not true

00:09:27.643 --> 00:09:29.793
so you don't know if you
can trust your parents.

00:09:30.887 --> 00:09:31.837
- And what was it like?

00:09:31.837 --> 00:09:35.760
I mean there's this
stereotype of people who,

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.050
child actors, they hit
notoriety early in their lives,

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.900
maybe their identity gets caught up in it

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:43.200
and you're kind of clinging onto it

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.210
and life throws interesting things at you.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:46.070
How did you deal?

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:47.550
Were you conscientious of that

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:51.623
when you were 10, 11, 12, 13 years old?

00:09:53.242 --> 00:09:56.050
- Yeah I mean because I come from,

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:56.990
at least for us,

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:58.750
I come from an immigrant mentality

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:01.480
where like you go to
school, you get an education

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:03.300
and I always had this notion

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.480
that like everything is fleeting,

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.740
the success is fleeting, like everything.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.983
It's a very Zen Buddhist philosophy,

00:10:10.862 --> 00:10:12.430
this too shall pass.

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:14.990
And so my education was
very important to me.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.210
It was very important to my
parents who were both teachers.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.360
They didn't want me being on a set

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:20.660
for many months of the year

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:24.460
to take the place of the
education that I still needed.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:26.850
So they went to a lot of work.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:28.680
There was a woman named Janie Teller

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.140
who was in charge of my education

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:33.160
and hired me different
tutors for every subject

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:37.320
and got me some incredible
really amazing tutors,

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:38.860
especially for my science subjects.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:40.990
And that's how I fell
in love with science.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:43.830
So I really, I was never really taken

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.659
with like the fame and the
money parts of show business.

00:10:47.659 --> 00:10:51.940
I was raised as a kind
of small intellectual.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:54.440
That's just how my dad
and my mom raised me.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:57.610
So I knew I wanted to be around people

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:59.010
who were thinking deeply

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.260
and while there are many
intelligent people in the industry,

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:06.270
I really thrived in a
public university system.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:09.160
I went to UCLA and yes,
it was intimidating

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:10.626
to be in large classes,

00:11:10.626 --> 00:11:12.250
for a lot of our core classes,

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.240
we were often with 300
people in the class,

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.960
but I made most of the

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:18.450
you're then divided into sections

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:23.330
and I made use of office hours
and I loved that environment.

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:26.640
I'm a very organized person
when it comes to schoolwork.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.900
So everything was color coded

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.210
and my assignment notebooks
and all those things

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:34.160
and I really just, I wanted
more of a normal life

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.013
than I think the industry
could provide me with.

00:11:38.091 --> 00:11:39.320
- And there's questions,

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.500
a ton of questions are coming in

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.730
off of YouTube and Facebook
and this is related.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:46.367
Crystal Gross writes from YouTube,

00:11:46.367 --> 00:11:48.920
"What is your advice for kids
who want to become scientists

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:49.880
or have careers in math

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.000
but they struggle with
courses geared towards that?

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.370
They have the dream, but not the grades."

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:55.830
And you had mentioned,
and you just mentioned

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:58.060
that at least initially you
were struggling a little bit

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:01.150
or maybe you felt a little
bit of an imposter syndrome.

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.400
How do you navigate that?

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.883
- I think part of it is,

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:10.530
there's two components for me.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:11.490
There's two components.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:14.280
One was the skill set and
the other was the confidence.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:19.280
And for me, kind of the
confidence had to come first,

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.080
meaning believing that I had the right

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.190
to learn these kinds of subjects

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:27.970
and that there's not just
one way to learn them.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:32.430
So for me, working with a
tutor was extremely helpful,

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:36.490
having more of an intimate
kind of one-on-one experience

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:38.720
was a real help for me.

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:39.970
In the time that I grew up,

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:44.010
there were not a lot of women in STEM,

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.360
at least in ways that I had access to.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:49.410
So having a female tutor
was also very empowering

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:52.780
because I could see someone
else who had been working at it.

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:54.340
She was 19 when I met her

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:56.490
and I was like, yeah, 15,

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.560
but you know, she was in college

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.060
and she came from a family,

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.130
an immigrant family from Iran

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.350
and education was very
important to her family

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:06.600
and all her sisters got to go

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.410
to either med school or dental school.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:10.240
So she's now an oral surgeon,

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.470
but having that relationship

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.690
where someone was teaching
me the way I learned

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:15.770
was really helpful.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:17.150
And I think also

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.940
the pacing of how classes
were traditionally taught

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:22.080
didn't always work for
me in those subjects.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:23.740
You know, English I could get like that,

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.030
like that wasn't a problem,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:27.380
sociology, anthropology, psychology,

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.980
all that stuff, that wasn't the issue.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.240
Numbers and formulas was hard for me

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.250
at the pacing that things were set at.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:35.860
So I did need to work harder.

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:38.010
And honestly, I had to work harder

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.380
all the way through my undergrad

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.060
and an all the way into grad school,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.260
meaning there were certain things

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.200
that came a lot easier to me,

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.110
but I worked in a genetics lab,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:48.990
conversions were always hard for me,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.960
even working in a genetics lab.

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:53.923
So there were still things that
I had to always keep up on.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.240
- And what was it,

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.880
what was the moment that
you said, neuroscience,

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:00.680
I want to be a neuroscientist,

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:02.370
even though it might have some of these

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.540
speed bumps along the way
that you might not have felt

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:08.167
are like your, wasn't the
easiest thing for you?

00:14:09.508 --> 00:14:10.440
- So the first thing

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.930
that really kind of
captivated me in science

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:18.930
was really cell biology, DNA,

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:20.590
like once I got to understand

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:21.780
and like once it clicked,

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:26.330
like, oh, it's this and then
the histones and the things,

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:27.270
I hope that's still true,

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.330
I mean I haven't studied DNA.

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:29.547
- It is true.

00:14:29.547 --> 00:14:32.493
I think histones are still
part of the packaging of DNA.

00:14:32.493 --> 00:14:33.326
- Proteins, yeah.

00:14:33.326 --> 00:14:36.470
So I was interested in
nature versus nurture,

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:38.340
which was a popular,

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.660
kind of a popular science
topic in the '90s,

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:41.910
like what makes us who we are?

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:43.820
I know for young people, it's like, duh,

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.740
but back then there was a
lot of discussion about it.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:48.510
And the God's honest truth

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:52.610
is that I wanted to study
biology when I got to UCLA,

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:55.800
but because my calculus and my chemistry

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.010
were not at an appropriately high level

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.390
I had to take really remedial,

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:05.110
I mean it was remedial
chemistry and remedial calculus.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.770
And so until I did those things

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:09.100
I couldn't register for biology.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:10.320
So I was so frustrated,

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:11.330
I was so embarrassed

00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:13.040
that like here I was
wanting to be a scientist

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:14.980
and I couldn't even start
the core curriculum,

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.260
but one of the classes that didn't require

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.190
my chemistry and calculus to be completed

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:22.990
was a class called Psychobiology.

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.510
And the first half of the class

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:27.150
was taught by a psychology professor

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:30.960
who specialized in things
like conditioned response,

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.460
like Pavlovian stuff,

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:34.340
very interesting, very important,

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:35.920
attachment theory, great.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:37.170
The second half of the course

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.520
was taught by Professor Iran Zidell

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.840
and he was in the psychology department

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:46.130
but he taught the neurophysiology
of the nervous system,

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:48.580
and in particular electrophysiology.

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:50.380
And when I saw, this is my moment,

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.840
when I saw that action potential chart

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:55.510
and I saw that electrical impulses

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:59.230
are what allow for the release
of sodium and potassium

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:00.280
and all of these ions

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:02.450
that lead to a chain reaction

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.500
that caused further electrical responses

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:07.130
and that's how we perceive the world,

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:09.450
I literally said, "That's the level.

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:12.120
I want to understand the
universe at that, that level."

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:16.510
And that is when I decided
to become a neuroscientist.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:19.030
- I am so excited to hear you say that.

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:21.710
I think we're gonna take that
clip of what you just said

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.770
and on Khan Academy where we introduce

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.180
action potential sodium potassium pump-

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.710
- I will teach that class with joy.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:30.710
I've done it before,

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:32.740
I would love to.

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:34.500
- And even just your answer right now.

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:36.337
The number one thing is people,

00:16:36.337 --> 00:16:37.480
I always tell folks

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:40.106
that you get to learn
all of this stuff that,

00:16:40.106 --> 00:16:41.290
what's in the textbook

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.550
is hundreds of years of human knowledge

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:45.490
and the people who dedicated their lives

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:46.720
to discovering what's in the textbook

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:48.640
would have killed to have that textbook.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.106
But so many kids are
like, "Ugh, I got to learn

00:16:51.106 --> 00:16:51.939
about the sodium potassium pump."

00:16:51.939 --> 00:16:55.030
But when they hear you out of all of what,

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.460
you were on "MacGyver",
that sounds exciting

00:16:57.460 --> 00:17:00.570
but I heard more excitement in your voice

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.126
about the sodium potassium and
action potentials in neurons

00:17:03.126 --> 00:17:05.020
than "MacGyver".

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:07.610
- Well, and also, I had the opportunity,

00:17:07.610 --> 00:17:09.170
I taught neuroscience,

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:10.150
I designed a curriculum

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.540
for junior high and high school students

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:12.650
in our homeschool community

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:14.320
and I taught for about five years,

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:15.812
before "Big Bang Theory,

00:17:15.812 --> 00:17:17.860
I taught in our community.

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:21.290
And to be able to be
a science communicator

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.095
is a very special,

00:17:23.095 --> 00:17:23.928
and you know, obviously,

00:17:23.928 --> 00:17:25.700
it's one thing to know the material

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:29.470
but to be either gifted or taught

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:32.330
the skills to communicate
it to other people

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:36.760
and to explain it until
you see that they get it,

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:38.210
it is an exceptional,

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:42.500
it's an exceptional moment of
like a microcosm of creation.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:47.430
You're creating an inspired thought

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:49.770
in someone who previously
didn't understand it.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:51.830
And when I think of the concepts
that were hardest for me,

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:53.820
I remember learning the optical system

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:56.800
and the way the ganglion neurons

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:58.820
and the amacrine neurons and
all those things line up,

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:00.130
it is so difficult

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:02.370
and how it's like a
constantly generating system

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:03.980
that needs to have a
negative feedback loop

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:05.280
and I remember how hard it was,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:06.900
but when you get it,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:09.710
that feeling, it's better than applause,

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:11.470
it's better than an Emmy.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:12.360
I've never won an Emmy,

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:14.080
I've only been nominated four times,

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.200
but that satisfaction

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:19.970
of the human mind grasping
something of that enormity

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:21.900
there literally is nothing like it.

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:25.240
I mean giving birth was
enormously significant

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.923
but it's on that level of like wow.

00:18:29.017 --> 00:18:31.480
- Well, no, you've made
me feel better 'cause I,

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:33.557
I haven't given birth,

00:18:33.557 --> 00:18:34.390
I've modeled it in my mind,

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:37.950
but I have had that
experience which you have

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:39.640
which is when something clicks

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:42.732
and you understand the
universe in another way

00:18:42.732 --> 00:18:45.710
like that is what it means to be human,

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:47.985
that is what it means to experience life.

00:18:47.985 --> 00:18:49.226
- Absolutely.

00:18:49.226 --> 00:18:50.476
- And I know you might be

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:52.940
saying that you would love to teach,

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:54.250
we will talk later.

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:57.020
We're doing a big push on
science on Khan Academy.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:59.520
- You know where to find me.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:01.970
- I know where to find you for sure.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:03.450
So all sorts of questions coming in.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.140
Actually before I get to a
more questions, I am curious,

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.360
when you were at UCLA or into grad school

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:10.720
and you're learning the neuroscience,

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:12.720
you're thinking about
becoming a neuroscientist,

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:13.740
was in the back of your mind,

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.920
like education's important,
it's a safety net,

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:18.480
that's my backup

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:22.440
in case Hollywood isn't
what what I hope it could be

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:23.470
or was it the other way around

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:24.467
like no I want to be a neuroscientist

00:19:24.467 --> 00:19:27.690
and on the side, I'll
do a couple of cameos

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.790
or be in a couple of shoes here in there.

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:31.420
- No, when I left the industry,

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:33.460
I was 19, I was two
years out of high school.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:36.980
I went to a very college prep style,

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:40.330
honestly junior high and high
school here in Los Angeles

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.910
and I was geared to go to college.

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:46.825
And to be honest, when
I left the industry,

00:19:46.825 --> 00:19:50.440
I didn't have active
plans to return to it.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:53.140
I wanted to live in an apartment

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:57.040
and walk to campus and
meet other students.

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.980
I was very active in the
Jewish Student Union.

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:03.140
I studied Japanese history
and I studied philosophy,

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.530
like I had the best time there

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:08.770
and I met the person that I
then married and had children.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:10.230
We met in calculus class.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:12.810
Like I was living a
totally different life.

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:14.720
I would go to school in pajamas

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:16.330
like most science students did

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.990
like I loved my life.

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:21.062
And I originally wanted
to go to med school.

00:20:21.062 --> 00:20:24.130
I wanted to study psychiatry,

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:29.120
God's honest truth, I could
not get higher than a B minus

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:30.360
in organic chemistry

00:20:31.626 --> 00:20:34.760
'cause that really was the
weeder class for med school

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:37.530
and I hate to say those
who can't go to med school,

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:39.750
go to grad school, but I did,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.650
I made the decision to be a
more of a research academic.

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:45.678
I was not planning on
going back to the industry.

00:20:45.678 --> 00:20:49.460
Like I said I taught after
getting my doctorate.

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:51.290
Acting is a very interesting world,

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:54.350
but it's not intellectually stimulating

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:56.950
the way being a scientist was.

00:20:56.950 --> 00:20:58.710
And to be perfectly honest,

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:00.850
you know, having my first son,

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:04.759
which I did after my data
collection for my thesis project

00:21:04.759 --> 00:21:05.592
and I essentially wrote my thesis

00:21:05.592 --> 00:21:07.493
while laying down breastfeeding,

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.170
it was the decision to leave academia

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:12.480
because I was choosing
to be home with my son

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:16.290
that actually shifted kind
of the trajectory of my life.

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:18.000
And that's a very difficult decision.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:21.590
I can't say it was the
right decision for everyone,

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:23.180
but for me it was the decision,

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:24.700
and for my husband at the time.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:26.120
that was the decision we made.

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:27.690
And so I tutored

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:29.330
and I also taught piano,

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:30.163
I taught Hebrew,

00:21:30.163 --> 00:21:33.550
I did a lot of things while
also being the primary caregiver

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.050
for my son

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:38.780
and then had another one right
after I got my doctorate.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:42.040
So my life really shifted in
terms of the academic track,

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:43.750
but I was still teaching.

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:46.470
And because of the United
States healthcare system,

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:50.520
I did return to acting to
literally do enough work

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.300
so that I could get my insurance back

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:53.420
through Screen Actors Guild,

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:54.880
which is excellent insurance.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:57.620
- Really, you acted for insurance?

00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:00.460
That's the most pragmatic
reason I've ever heard.

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:01.840
- Well and someone said,

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:04.120
we literally got Mayim
on the "Big Bang Theory"

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:07.333
because of the failure
of our healthcare system.

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:13.550
- Y'all must have better
healthcare than I imagined

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:15.838
at the Screen Actors Guild.

00:22:15.838 --> 00:22:17.380
That's good, I'm happy to hear that.

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:22.033
Talking to you is fascinating

00:22:22.033 --> 00:22:23.450
because I listed three things,

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:25.100
author, actress, neuroscientist,

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:27.350
but clearly mother,

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:29.930
very important, very
large role of your life.

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:33.970
Now you are also doing this
podcast, "Bialik Breakdown",

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:35.420
which is about mental health.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:39.890
You are this polymath type person,

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:42.250
you're taking on a lot
of different projects.

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:47.250
Why the podcast now and why
this focus on mental health?

00:22:47.308 --> 00:22:50.790
- You know, my interest
has really always hovered

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:52.830
around mental health challenges.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:55.180
I grew up in a home
that had mental illness,

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:56.640
as many homes do.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:59.880
And I think many families have it

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:01.590
but we don't really call it that

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:03.920
or even when I listen to stories

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:06.200
of my grandparent's generation,

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:08.970
oh this one was like, and it's
like, that was schizophrenia.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:10.980
You know, we didn't know to call it that

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.910
but someone who hears voices,

00:23:12.910 --> 00:23:15.780
like that's actually a
clinical diagnosis, you know?

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:18.150
So I have always had an interest

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:21.450
in the way we react to those environments

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.920
and what's genetic and
what's environmental.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.090
I do have my share of challenges

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:27.740
and I've been very open about it,

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.730
but it was really last year

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.150
when the world got very
small in many ways,

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:33.660
as many of us were home,

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.840
that so many people that I
knew started experiencing

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:39.170
in particular anxiety and panic

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:42.340
and they honestly didn't
even know what to call it.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:43.650
And I was thinking how many people

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:45.450
are experiencing challenges

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:47.740
that are lacking just
the basic information

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:49.740
about what's going on.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:51.310
So my partner, Jonathan and I,

00:23:51.310 --> 00:23:53.700
we started a "Mayim Bialik's Breakdown"

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:57.210
really to start breaking
down some of the terms

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:59.770
and some of the misconceptions also

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:01.400
that people have about mental health.

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:03.540
And this isn't like
I've figured it all out,

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:04.530
come and listen

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:06.520
how I'm gonna tell you
to make your life better.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:08.940
This is I still live with anxiety,

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:10.580
I have struggled with depression,

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:12.680
I am on the OCD spectrum.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:14.600
Those are just true things about me.

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:16.470
And I still get to figure out

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:17.570
what works and what doesn't.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:20.320
And the notion of mind body syndrome

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:24.000
and the things that Eastern
medicine and Eastern cultures

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:25.460
have known about for thousands of years

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:27.410
that we're finally catching onto

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:28.810
is also something that

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:30.620
that Jonathan are looking to explore,

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:33.240
meaning there is science to acupuncture.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.060
There is science to why certain smells

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.990
do certain things to your brain.

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:39.160
Those are real things.

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:41.680
And if there are things that we can do

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:42.920
before we get to let's say

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.510
medication and diagnoses and DSM this,

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:47.430
we should all know about those things.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:49.133
I think it should be a human right

00:24:49.133 --> 00:24:50.550
for us to be educated

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.210
about how our mind is
connected to our body

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:54.950
and how that impacts our mental health.

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.480
So we talk to experts in
the field every episode

00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:59.120
or people who are experts

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:00.770
in their own journey
and their own struggles.

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:03.080
And sometimes those
are famous celebrities,

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:06.280
but it is available on Spotify
or wherever you get podcasts

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:08.690
and we also do broadcast
it on my YouTube channel,

00:25:08.690 --> 00:25:10.230
which has almost a million followers.

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.090
So we encourage people also to sign up

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.370
if you'd like to see me and Jonathan.

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:15.320
We wear matching outfits every episode.

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:17.993
- I didn't catch that

00:25:17.993 --> 00:25:19.350
that you wear matching outfits.

00:25:20.881 --> 00:25:21.787
No I mean it's a very important thing.

00:25:21.787 --> 00:25:24.260
And I have to say that I
was one of these people,

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:26.253
you know, we grew up in a culture where,

00:25:28.445 --> 00:25:31.580
you kind of take pride in
oh, I'm functional, I'm sane,

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:33.240
et cetera, et cetera and then you're like,

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:35.060
these are other people's problems.

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:36.350
And I think, as you know,

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:38.760
every family has a lot in it

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:40.310
that usually you try to hide

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:43.838
and not have transparent
to the rest of the world.

00:25:43.838 --> 00:25:45.577
And I mentioned it on your podcast

00:25:45.577 --> 00:25:47.500
and I think I've mentioned it here too,

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:51.640
is my first real glimpse personally

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:53.830
was about five years ago where I think

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:56.960
frankly because of stress
at work and other things,

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:58.720
I started getting
incredibly claustrophobic,

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:00.230
I wasn't sleeping as much as,

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:02.180
I found myself going for
runs at three in the morning

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:03.090
because that's all I could do

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:05.983
to get rid of some of my energy,

00:26:05.983 --> 00:26:08.740
and that was the first
time that I realized

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:11.010
that I shouldn't just like identify myself

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:12.190
as a functional person,

00:26:12.190 --> 00:26:14.590
that we all have, and it creeps up on you,

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:15.423
it creeps up on you,

00:26:15.423 --> 00:26:17.060
it's like a frog in boiling water.

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:18.930
And so it's really important,

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:19.950
for me what I mentioned yesterday,

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:20.890
for me, meditation.

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:23.470
And when I meditated
almost out of desperation

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:25.490
to not get claustrophobic in planes,

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:28.100
which clearly I had to
travel a lot for my job,

00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:30.060
it opened up a whole new world for me.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:32.280
So not only did it help solve

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:34.190
the claustrophobia and the anxiety

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:36.420
and the panic I would get in small spaces,

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:37.860
but it helped open my life

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.403
in other really powerful, beautiful ways

00:26:40.403 --> 00:26:41.236
that I would never have done

00:26:41.236 --> 00:26:43.130
had I not experienced that.

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:47.355
So happy that you are taking this mantle,

00:26:47.355 --> 00:26:49.833
people should be talking
about this because it,

00:26:51.010 --> 00:26:52.340
when we talk about mental health,

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:54.640
people always talk about from
the deficit side of things,

00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:58.710
but it can also add and
gives a space for it.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.870
So in the time we have left,

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:04.180
and there's so much so many questions

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.310
that are coming up about
all all sorts of things,

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:08.613
maybe from Nich786,

00:27:09.497 --> 00:27:11.000
"What are some challenges you faced

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:12.510
becoming a neuroscientist?

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:13.800
What advice do you have for people

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:15.340
who want to follow a path like yours?"

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:16.173
You mentioned a few of them,

00:27:16.173 --> 00:27:17.690
but I'm sure there were others.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:20.000
- Yeah I mean,

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:21.720
honestly being a woman

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:26.544
in a field that is typically
male can have its challenges.

00:27:26.544 --> 00:27:31.544
There are women in situations
far far more challenging

00:27:32.213 --> 00:27:34.140
than what I experienced

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.550
and what many women experience.

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:38.680
But I think it is definitely
something to acknowledge

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:41.820
that the standards for women

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:46.820
tend to be very limited in terms of,

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:50.760
there's a few ways that
people are often comfortable

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:52.970
with women being in the sciences.

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:55.010
And so that's something just to kind of,

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:56.470
that you have to keep in mind.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:00.380
I think also, deciding when
and how to have children

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:01.560
was particularly challenging.

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:03.620
And not every woman wants to have children

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:05.860
or there are a lot of ways to be a mom

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:07.520
without physically giving birth,

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:10.820
but it's definitely a set of challenges

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:14.360
that I had to kind of plan and
time the best that I could.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:19.360
And I'm not a great test taker
and there are exams required,

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:21.330
there was the GRE,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:23.240
which was very challenging,

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:24.740
to get into grad school,

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:28.420
and then we had qualifying
exams our first year,

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:30.370
two of which I needed
to do remediation on,

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:31.390
which was humiliating,

00:28:31.390 --> 00:28:33.673
but also just part of the process.

00:28:34.589 --> 00:28:37.830
I was called into the head
of the department's office

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:40.680
after my first year and she said, "We",

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:41.857
you know, all of neuroscience,

00:28:41.857 --> 00:28:43.850
"we're kind of surprised and disappointed

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:45.420
'cause you were an A student

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:47.850
and yet you did not do
well on your exams."

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:49.413
And I kind of hung my head,

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.480
it was definitely hard.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:55.860
And I think that's also just
cause it wasn't something

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:59.470
that came naturally naturally
to me, that kind of thinking.

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:01.401
So those were,

00:29:01.401 --> 00:29:02.234
you know, I cried in her office,

00:29:02.234 --> 00:29:06.163
it was not a pretty moment,
but I made it through.

00:29:09.121 --> 00:29:10.576
- I mean, were there moments

00:29:10.576 --> 00:29:12.266
where you thought you
were gonna just give up

00:29:12.266 --> 00:29:14.540
or was there a voice saying
no you're not gonna give up?

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:17.260
- Yeah, I mean I think
anyone who's done a PhD,

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:20.260
it's like literally people forget also,

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:23.810
I did a PhD when there
wasn't really computer access

00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:25.690
to articles or information.

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:28.000
We had to physically go to the library,

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:29.670
which at UCLA, we call it the Stacks,

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:32.590
and it's like a 10 story building

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:33.950
and you would get a little card

00:29:33.950 --> 00:29:35.330
that you would have to put cash on

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:36.710
so that you can make the copies

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:38.630
and you'd be in the middle of some article

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:40.280
and then you'd run out
of money on your card

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:41.240
and by the time you got back,

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:43.660
someone shelved the book
that you were working on.

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:45.930
So it was a lot of physical labor

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:50.430
and also formatting a 300 page
thesis is also no small feat.

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:53.540
And we also didn't have all the tools

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:55.280
that are now available to do that.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:58.440
The formatting alone made me
want to not get my doctorate.

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:02.040
Like that was the level of
proficiency I felt I had

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:03.070
with that aspect.

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:04.860
So I know those sound silly

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:07.670
but when you've been doing
research for six years

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:09.490
and you're just trying to finish

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:11.440
and you have a newborn,

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:13.260
the last thing you want to be told

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:15.060
by the office where you submit

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:18.520
is that your margins are off on page three

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:21.613
and you have to start all
over with your formatting.

00:30:22.653 --> 00:30:24.900
- I just can imagine the thoughts.

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:26.570
I mean I actually can't imagine

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:29.240
'cause one of my friends from college,

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:31.670
actually I've known him
from childhood, he was,

00:30:31.670 --> 00:30:32.503
I got to know him,

00:30:32.503 --> 00:30:33.580
he beat me the math competition,

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:34.990
so I'm like, I'm gonna
hang out with this guy.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:36.340
He beat me in 10th grade

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:38.430
and then he actually ended
up being the first president,

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:40.710
like the COO and president of
Khan Academy when we started,

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.831
so very close friend of mine.

00:30:42.831 --> 00:30:47.362
But I remember when,
and he, super smart guy,

00:30:47.362 --> 00:30:49.910
but I remember, he had his first child

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:53.160
a year before I had mine or we had ours

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:55.050
and I remember one day he was just like,

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:56.566
his hair is all disheveled,

00:30:56.566 --> 00:30:57.820
he's like, "Sal, I don't know,

00:30:57.820 --> 00:31:00.064
everything else has been kind of easy.

00:31:00.064 --> 00:31:01.900
I can crush tests."

00:31:01.900 --> 00:31:03.260
I mean he is a good test taker.

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:04.859
He's like, "It's just been easy."

00:31:04.859 --> 00:31:06.880
He was a triple major at MIT, it was easy.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:11.880
He's like, "But this
child rearing, it's hard

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:14.330
and everyone does it.

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:16.450
How come everyone does it?

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:18.800
If it's so hard for me,
how can everyone else?"

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:20.993
And he's right.

00:31:20.993 --> 00:31:24.413
But the fact that you were
doing that while getting a PhD-

00:31:26.467 --> 00:31:28.420
- He's a total nerd, my first,

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:29.280
they're both kind of nerdy,

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:31.330
but my first born, he was literally,

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:33.273
he was raised on thesis.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:35.887
- There might be something to that.

00:31:35.887 --> 00:31:36.820
You know, people like play Mozart

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:39.808
while their kids are
like if you do a thesis,

00:31:39.808 --> 00:31:41.471
if you're at the copying machine

00:31:41.471 --> 00:31:43.130
while they're getting the radiation,

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:44.353
it might do something.

00:31:45.585 --> 00:31:48.490
- Just as, maybe to finish off,

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:49.750
a lot of people asking a lot of questions,

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:51.540
your views of the future, 2021,

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:53.480
what are you looking forward to?

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.820
You have so many projects,
so many interests.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:58.870
What do you kind of see as
the next phase of your life,

00:31:58.870 --> 00:32:01.013
next 10 years say?

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:05.223
- Gosh, you know, I
hope to retire by then.

00:32:06.254 --> 00:32:07.154
I'm just so tired.

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:12.480
You know, I'm 45 and
my kids are 12 and 15.

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.270
So in many ways,

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:18.210
my entire life is kind of centered around

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:20.860
where they are in their
academics and in their life,

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:22.810
like my younger one's
preparing for his bar mitzvah.

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:25.681
So I'm tutoring him for that.

00:32:25.681 --> 00:32:28.010
My older son will likely be starting

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:29.890
online community college courses,

00:32:29.890 --> 00:32:32.660
that's what a lot of
homeschool high schoolers do.

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:37.580
So I think of where they
will be in the next 10 years.

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:39.580
I definitely, I am working on a new show.

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:40.920
It's called "Call Me Cat",

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:42.190
and it's fun,

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:43.880
I'm having a really great time doing it.

00:32:43.880 --> 00:32:47.490
So I'm hoping that we get
a second season of that.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:49.370
And honestly, this podcast is really,

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:50.660
it's really my passion.

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:53.720
You know, it sounds so
pretentious, but it is,

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:55.920
it is my passion to
talk about mental health

00:32:55.920 --> 00:33:00.050
and to be able to maybe
one day be able to advocate

00:33:00.050 --> 00:33:04.580
on a larger DC level for
mental health access.

00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:06.790
That's something I would
really, really love to see

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:09.580
as what I get to work on.

00:33:09.580 --> 00:33:14.580
But honestly, I miss traveling and it's,

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:18.200
I'm very conservative I
guess when it comes to COVID,

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:20.430
as many scientists tend to be.

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:22.860
So I'm also really just kind of cautious

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:24.590
about what this year is gonna look like.

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:26.030
And I think a lot of people

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:28.270
don't understand the
importance of the vaccine.

00:33:28.270 --> 00:33:29.840
I think a lot of people don't understand

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:31.890
the implications of not
getting the vaccine.

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:33.120
I've been very vocal about that.

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:36.230
I put out a YouTube video
about the importance of that.

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:40.070
So I'm kind of cautious about
what this year will look like

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:41.260
because it's been a long time

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:42.967
since I've been to a supermarket

00:33:42.967 --> 00:33:44.680
and I'm ready to get back out there,

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:46.053
but just waiting.

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:48.983
- No, I definitely hear you.

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:52.050
I live with obviously my
wife and my mother-in-law.

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:52.990
My wife's a doctor,

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:55.010
and so she's gotten her vaccine now.

00:33:55.010 --> 00:33:56.500
My mother-in-law just got her booster.

00:33:56.500 --> 00:33:58.380
So I am now the major
risk factor in the house.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:00.070
So I'm gonna see how
much they care about me.

00:34:00.070 --> 00:34:01.600
- I'm sure it's not the first time

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:04.340
you've been the most dangerous
person in your house.

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:05.173
- That's right.

00:34:05.173 --> 00:34:06.006
That's right.

00:34:06.006 --> 00:34:07.351
I like that.

00:34:07.351 --> 00:34:08.559
I'm glad you see that in me.

00:34:08.559 --> 00:34:10.570
So Mayim, thank you so,

